tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: The mechanical clock and the verge escapement", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Belfry with weight, drum, crown wheel, verge and foliot, bell and dial.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Medieval Europe (with Chinese and Islamic water-clock precursors), weight-driven escapement clocks from the 1280s-1300s", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "A falling weight turns a gear train; the verge-and-foliot escapement lets the train", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "advance one tooth at a time, converting the weight's steady pull into counted beats. Su", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Song's Chinese water-driven astronomical clock (1092) had an escapement too, but water-", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "driven.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "A crown wheel with saw teeth is checked by two pallets on a vertical rod (the verge); a", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "horizontal bar with adjustable weights (the foliot) swings back and forth, each swing", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "releasing one tooth. Accuracy was poor (15 minutes a day) until the pendulum (1656), but", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "the hour was now mechanical and public, struck on a bell.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:london/westminster", "color": "aqua"}
